---
import ServicePage from '../layouts/ServicePage.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { serviceBySlug } from '../data/services';
const service = serviceBySlug('vaccination-information')!;

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Do I need to book a vaccination?', a: 'No. Walk in any day we’re open and scan the QR code at the counter to join the queue. If you’d rather pick a time, book online.' },
  { q: 'Is there a service fee for free government vaccines?', a: 'No. Vaccines funded under the National Immunisation Program are free for eligible people with a Medicare card, and we don’t charge a fee to give them. Bring your Medicare card and we’ll check your eligibility.' },
  { q: 'Can I have more than one vaccine at the same visit?', a: 'Often, yes. Tell us what’s due and your pharmacist will check which vaccines can be given together.' },
  { q: 'What if I feel unwell on the day?', a: 'A mild cold usually isn’t a problem. If you have a fever, wait until you’re well. Not sure? Call us and ask.' },
  { q: 'Are there side effects?', a: 'A sore arm, tiredness or a mild temperature for a day or two are common. If you’re worried about any reaction after leaving the store, contact your GP. In an emergency, call 000.' },
  { q: 'How long does a vaccination take?', a: 'Allow about 20 minutes. The vaccination itself is quick, and we’ll ask you to stay in store for 15 minutes afterwards. Walk in, or book a time that suits.' },
  { q: 'Will my vaccination be recorded?', a: 'Yes. Every vaccination we give is recorded on the Australian Immunisation Register, so your GP and your myGov immunisation history stay up to date.' },
];
---

<ServicePage
  service={service}
  title="Vaccinations at Pacific Fair, Broadbeach | Walk-ins Welcome"
  description="Walk in for flu, COVID-19, whooping cough, shingles and travel vaccines at Priceline Pacific Fair, Broadbeach. Free NIP vaccines, no service fee, ages 5+."
  h1="Vaccinations at Pacific Fair, Broadbeach"
  lead="Vaccinations at Priceline Pharmacy Pacific Fair in Broadbeach need no appointment: walk in any day we’re open. Our pharmacist immunisers give flu, COVID-19, whooping cough, shingles, pneumococcal and travel vaccines to adults and children aged 5 and over, in a private consultation area. National Immunisation Program vaccines are free for eligible people with no service fee; walk in, or book a time online."
  faqs={faqs}
  hub={{ name: 'Pharmacy services', href: '/pharmacy-services/' }}
  primaryCta={{ label: 'Book a vaccination time', href: site.links.book, external: true, event: 'cta_book' }}
  secondaryCta={{ label: 'Walk in: today’s hours', href: '/visit-us/' }}
  dateModified="2026-10-07"
>
  <h2>Which vaccines are free?</h2>
  <p>Vaccines under the National Immunisation Program (NIP) are free for eligible people with a Medicare card, and we don’t charge a service fee to give them. Eligibility depends on your age, pregnancy, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander status and some medical conditions. The main groups are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flu:</strong> children aged 6 months to under 5, pregnant women, people aged 65 and over, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people with certain medical conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Whooping cough:</strong> pregnant women, ideally between 20 and 32 weeks of each pregnancy.</li>
    <li><strong>Shingles:</strong> people aged 65 and over, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people aged 50 and over, and people aged 18 and over at higher risk because of a medical condition or treatment.</li>
    <li><strong>Pneumococcal:</strong> people aged 65 and over, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people aged 25 and over, and adults with certain medical conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>COVID-19:</strong> free for everyone in Australia, in line with current advice.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Not sure if you’re eligible? Ask us and we’ll check for you, or see the <a href="https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-immunisation-program" rel="noopener">National Immunisation Program</a>. If you’re not eligible, a private fee applies; you’ll see current prices when you book online.</p>

  <h2>Which vaccines do you offer?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Influenza (flu). See our <a href="/flu-vaccination/">flu vaccination</a> page.</li>
    <li>COVID-19</li>
    <li>Whooping cough (diphtheria, tetanus and pertussis)</li>
    <li>Shingles</li>
    <li>Pneumococcal</li>
    <li>Human papillomavirus (HPV)</li>
    <li>Meningococcal ACWY and meningococcal B</li>
    <li>Measles, mumps and rubella (MMR)</li>
    <li>Chickenpox (varicella)</li>
    <li>Hepatitis A and hepatitis B</li>
    <li>Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib)</li>
    <li>Travel vaccines: typhoid, cholera, Japanese encephalitis and polio. See <a href="/travel-health/">travel health</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who can we vaccinate?</h2>
  <p>Our pharmacists vaccinate adults and children aged 5 and over. For younger children, the GPs at <a href="/help-medical/">Help Medical</a>, an independent general practice located inside our store, can help.</p>

  <h2>Do I need to book?</h2>
  <p>No. Walk in any day we’re open, or book a time if you prefer. Here’s how it works:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Walk in or book.</strong> Scan the QR code at the counter to join the queue, or <a href={site.links.book} rel="noopener">book a time online</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Quick check.</strong> We’ll ask a few questions about your health, allergies and previous vaccines.</li>
    <li><strong>Your vaccine.</strong> It’s given in our private consultation area.</li>
    <li><strong>Stay for 15 minutes.</strong> We’ll ask you to wait in store for 15 minutes afterwards, just in case of a reaction.</li>
    <li><strong>Recorded for you.</strong> Your vaccination is recorded on the Australian Immunisation Register.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What should I bring?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your Medicare card, if you have one</li>
    <li>Details of any recent vaccines, if you know them</li>
    <li>A list of your current medicines</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Which vaccines do I need for overseas travel?</h2>
  <p>It depends on where you’re going. Some travel vaccines need several doses or take time to work, so start planning 6 to 8 weeks before you fly if you can. See our <a href="/travel-health/">travel health</a> page, or book a travel consultation online.</p>

  <h2>Which vaccines are recommended in pregnancy?</h2>
  <p>Whooping cough and flu vaccines are recommended during pregnancy to protect you and your baby in the first months of life, and both are free under the NIP. Talk to your pharmacist, GP or midwife about timing, then walk in or book a time with us.</p>
</ServicePage>
